
# Build the Angular Library
ng build ng-dialog-sync --configuration production
# The --configuration production flag applies optimizations for publishing

After this command completes, you will find the publishable package contents in dist/ng-dialog-sync/.

# Update the Library's package.json
The package.json file inside dist/ng-dialog-sync/ is what gets published to npm. It's automatically generated by ng-packagr, but you should ensure the peerDependencies are correct.

Open projects/ng-dialog-sync/package.json (this is the one ng-packagr uses to generate the dist one) and ensure it has the correct peerDependencies for Angular and RxJS. I've already provided this in the ng-dialog-sync-package-json block above, but double-check it.

Important: The version in projects/ng-dialog-sync/package.json is what will be published. Make sure it's set to 1.0.0 for the first publish, and increment it for subsequent publishes.

# Publish Your Package to npm
Log in to npm:

Bash

npm login
(If you don't have an account, create one at npmjs.com.)

Navigate to the dist directory of your library:

Bash

cd dist/ng-dialog-sync
Publish the package:

Bash

npm publish
If you get a versioning error like "Cannot implicitly apply the 'latest' tag because previously published version X.Y.Z is higher...", it means you've already published that version or a higher one. Go back to projects/ng-dialog-sync/package.json, increment the version (e.g., 1.0.0 to 1.0.1), rebuild (ng build ng-dialog-sync --configuration production), and then try npm publish again.


# npm install ng-dialog-sync